                                                              "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEieF9ccr4palemNCYpPgnsfGVx5ac51S7c6CtbK3EvIgvQleNoLbELJ9sSoo5qWy8DG_DGj9hPOyJp2FV9emjGilWsDYxS4oxHVroIFMWZy1-ka1sbMW2jARxYOS5Phj5ADzYHZ0DjBTIlze-drCXEEe40iYQc1da-xUwtCJ3jUGkEKwEwRTBQ2mTOLYgw/w335-h400/BigMarketDelusion.jpg",
                                                              "summary":  "Damodaran argues AI has crossed from speculative hype into a phase demanding real business answers: revenue, profitability, and defensible market sizing. Current AI product revenues of approximately $250 billion are modest against trillions invested, and he caps the realistic total addressable market at $12.96 trillion ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â equal to total US employee compensation. Key variables include whether AI acts as a productivity tool or a workforce replacement, pricing power, industry penetration, and geographic reach. He applies a reverse-engineering framework to AI company valuations, questioning whether metrics like Anthropic\u0027s implied revenue multiples reflect rational expectations or residual hype.",
                                                              "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s $12.96 trillion TAM ceiling is the most rigorous constraint I\u0027ve seen applied to AI market sizing, and it matters enormously for portfolio positioning. Most AI bull cases implicitly assume markets far beyond total labor compensation ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a mathematical impossibility unless AI creates entirely new economic categories. The valuation reverse-engineering approach he advocates is exactly what institutional investors should be doing with private AI unicorns before secondary market exposure. The practical read: be highly selective on AI pure-plays at current multiples; the margin of safety is thin and the runway to profitability remains long.",
