                                                              "summary":  "Brown interviews Big Technology\u0027s Alex Kantrowitz, arguing that mainstream investors are focused on current AI applications like chatbots while visionary investors are building infrastructure for a future of \u0027a billion people ordering around their own AI agents.\u0027 Tech leaders are reportedly less worried about data center overbuilding and more concerned that compute capacity will prove insufficient for autonomous vehicle, humanoid robot, and automated manufacturing demands. Brown concludes that massive compute expansion is not just likely but structurally inevitable given the trajectory of autonomous systems.",
                                                              "take":  "Brown\u0027s framing shifts the hyperscaler capex debate from \u0027overbuilding risk\u0027 to \u0027underbuilding risk\u0027 ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â a contrarian but defensible position if autonomous agent adoption follows the trajectories being modeled internally at the major labs. The key investment implication is that infrastructure picks (power, networking, custom silicon, data centers) may have a longer and steeper demand curve than consensus assumes. If autonomous agents genuinely scale to billions of concurrent users, today\u0027s capacity looks like a rounding error. That said, the timeline risk is real ÃƒÂ¢Ã¢â€šÂ¬Ã¢â‚¬Â infrastructure built for a 2028 demand wave that arrives in 2031 still destroys IRR.",
